--TEST--
`echo` and `print` refuse a `secret`-qualified operand, including one an interpolation or a `.` spread the qualifier to — `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s terminal-output sink
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s terminal bullet: output is the secret being *displayed to a
// person* rather than used, so it is refused with no `Core\Cli\Text` bypass —
// neutralizing a control byte does nothing for confidentiality. `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink`
// widens "the terminal" to a scheduled script's, a job worker's and a
// `#[Test]` method's captured output, which is where a credential leaks in
// practice.
string $plain = "plain";
tainted string $t = "t";
secret string $s = "s";
secret bytes $sb = "s" as bytes;
secret tainted string $st = "s";

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. `tainted` is not on this list: `rule:security/sink-predicate`
// wants a tainted value logged and displayed, and it is the HTML sink's
// escaping rather than the terminal that makes it safe.
echo $plain, "\n";
echo "interpolated {$t}\n";
print "printed\n";

// The bare binding, at both statements — `print` differs from `echo` only in
// having a value, which is nothing the qualifier cares about.
echo $s;
print $s;

// The operand is usually not the binding itself: interpolation and `.` spread
// the qualifier to their result (`rule:security/secret-qualifier`), so one check at the sink
// covers § 4's "`echo` and interpolation" both, and the span it names is the
// whole composed literal.
echo "Bearer {$s}\n";
echo "Bearer " . $s, "\n";

// The other base, and the two bits composed — `secret` is one axis and
// `tainted` never launders it. A `secret bytes` operand would also answer the
// "no string form" question, and does not: a refused operand is not asked it,
// because confidentiality is the half to act on.
echo $sb;
echo $st;
--EXPECT--

